// Book One, Chapter 17 (session 15). The day and dusk after the waking, in the Merchant house: the guest, the custom of surety, the Senate's summons.
// Two day actions of four places, then the dusk table and the first drink. No rest(); closes with => next.
// Chapter 18 makes the choice about the General (original_fate). This chapter only gathers what each side wants and settles who he will answer to.
@chapter b1c17
@title The Surety
@book burning-city
@version 1
@next b1c18

# start
## Under the roof of the Merchant house
$ do phase("day", 2)
$ b1.general_meal = "animal"
$ _room = 0
$ _asked = 0
$ _study = 0
$ _seen = 0
$ _lane = 0
$ _light = 0
$ do meet("general")
$ do discover("the_general")
You sleep in the end, in a clerk's cell behind the counting room, with a chest against the door and a hot brick at your feet that somebody has thought to put there. It is a very small kindness. When you wake it is the middle of the afternoon, with the shutters barred and slats of white light lying across the floor, and the whole of the Merchant house is making a noise that you have not heard it make before.
It is the noise of a house that is trying very hard to be quiet.
?if original("general", "by") == "bloodline"
  The General's line has come to the Via Labicana. They are in the lane outside, in the thin black shade of the wall, in twos and threes, in plain grey, and they have been there since the sun was high. They are not armed. They do not speak. Now and then one of them lifts his head, like a hound that has caught a scent, and puts it down again.
?elif original("general", "by") == "faction"
  The Lictors have come to the Via Labicana. They are in the lane outside, in two red files, with their cedar grounded like spears, and the officer in charge has told Hanno very politely that the Senate has posted them for the guest's protection. Hanno has said that it is a great comfort. He has said it without any inflection at all.
?else
  Everybody has come to the Via Labicana. The General's line is in the shade of the wall in grey twos and threes, and the Lictors are at the corner in two red files with their cedar grounded, and between them there is a stretch of empty road, a dozen paces long, that neither will cross. Both have been told by somebody that it is your doing. It is not a thing that you can contradict.
?end
Hanno is waiting outside the door of the clerk's cell, with a cup and a tablet. He has been waiting, you think, for some time. He gives you the cup. It is warm, and it is not wine, and it is not a thing that you ask about.
"The guest is in the strongroom," he says. It is the longest speech that you have ever had from him. "The lady will see you in the study. The Senate has sent a clerk. There is also," he considers, "a goat."
He does not explain the goat.
You have a day to spend, and a dusk after it, and the sense of a thing that is coming toward you down a long straight road. There are four places in the house or near it where you might be wanted.
-> day

# day
## The long afternoon
:day
?if actions_left() == 2
  The house is very still. Somewhere below there is the scrape of a chair on stone, and a murmur in a voice that you would know now anywhere, deep and creaking and fond of its own jokes.
?else
  The light has gone amber at the edges of the shutters. A servant goes by in the passage on bare feet, with a basin held out in front of her, carefully, like something that might go off.
?end
* Go down to the strongroom, where they have put the General [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> room
  $ do act()
* Go to Prisca, in her study [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> study
  $ do act()
* Go up the Caelian and answer the Senate's clerk [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> caelian
  $ do act()
* Go out into the lane, to the General's people [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> lane
  $ do act()
?if actions_left() <= 0
  $ do end_phase()
  -> dusk
?end
-> :day

# room
## The strongroom
$ _room += 1
$ do meet("meritamun")
The strongroom is at the bottom of the house, under the counting room, behind a door of oak and iron that has been kept, by an economy of the Merchant house that nobody has ever apologised for, a great deal more carefully than the doors upstairs. It is a vault for coin and for contracts. It has been swept. They have brought in a couch and a table and a brazier that gives no light, and Hanno has had a rug put down over the flags, and it is, you think, the saddest and most dignified prison that you have ever seen.
The General is sitting up on the couch with a cup in both hands. He has a Roman tunic on. It has been made, in a hurry, for a man a head shorter, and it ends at his knee and his wrist, and he has plainly decided to carry it off.
He is a little less like a skeleton. It is a matter of an inch of flesh across the cheekbone, and a faint tinge in the parchment of his skin. He looks up at you with the slow turning of that great attention, and for a moment it is like standing in front of a lantern. Then he smiles, and the effect is much less alarming than it ought to be.
"I have been given a sheep," he says. "I should like it understood that I am grateful. I should also like it understood that I have been given a sheep."
Meritamun is at the end of the table with a roll of papyrus half unwound and her reed behind her ear. She does not look up. She has the face of a woman who has been awake for forty hours and would not trade a minute of it. "He has been talking since dawn," she says. "I have been told that I may write it down, provided that I do not read it back to him."
"It is a matter of delicacy," says the General.
:room_ask
* Ask him how he is [once]
  "I am a very old man in a very small room," says the General cheerfully, "with a great number of people outside it who are afraid of me. It is a position that I have been in before. It was a good deal colder." He drinks from the cup, with a look of grave distaste. "The hunger is a thing that you understand. It does not hurt. It *attends*. One has forgotten how much of it there is, in a body that is awake."
  $ bond.general += 1
  $ _asked += 1
  -> :room_ask
* Ask him what was here before the city [once]
  He puts back his head and laughs. It is the same rusty, creaking, helpless sound as in the quarry, but quieter, and it goes on a long time. "A hill," he says. "Two hills, with a marsh between, and a very poor spring. There was a man with a plough. He drew a furrow in the dirt, very straight, and then he shouted at his brother, and then there was a quarrel, and I recall that I thought it was a poor way to begin." He wipes his eyes. "I was under the ground at the time. I felt it through the stone. I did not think that it would come to anything."
  Meritamun's reed is going. She says, without lifting her head, "I have the date of that. I had always supposed it to be a legend."
  "It was a quarrel about a wall," says the General. "They are always quarrels about a wall."
  $ bond.general += 1
  $ _asked += 1
  -> :room_ask
* Ask him who put him under the hill [once]
  The smile does not go. It goes still, and then it goes patient, and it is the most unsettling thing that you have yet seen on that face.
  "People who loved me," says the General. He says it as one gives the date of a battle. "It is the worst way to be put anywhere."
  He looks at the cup. It is a long moment. Meritamun's reed has stopped.
  "I will tell you the rest," he says, "when I have decided what the rest is. It is a courtesy that I am extending to myself." He lifts his eyes to yours, and the lantern is on again, bright and kind and entirely without mercy. "I do not think that you will mind. You do not seem to be a person who is in a hurry to be lied to."
  $ bond.general += 2
  $ _asked += 1
  -> :room_ask
* Tell him about the emperor, and the lyre [once]
  You tell him as plainly as you can. It does not sound any plainer for being told. You say that there is an emperor, and that he is young, and that he sings. You say that he has had his mother killed. You say that when the first of the fire took the Circus he was at his seaside house, and that some people say that he came back to watch it, and that a great many more say that he did not, and that you were there and do not know.
  The General listens with enormous attention and no expression at all. "Ah," he says at last, with a note of faint disappointment. "I had hoped that was a story." He considers. "I knew a king once who played the lyre. It is a thing that one should never let a man do who is also able to have people killed. It does not improve either activity."
  $ bond.general += 1
  $ _asked += 1
  -> :room_ask
* Ask him what he wants [once]
  "Ah," says the General. "That." He looks at the wall. "I have been asked that by a great many people with cedar in their hands. I find that it is not a question that wants an answer from a man who has just woken up." He turns his huge, wasted head, and looks at you. "Not yet. I should like to be given a little time to be someone, and then I will tell you what he wants."
  He says it so reasonably that it takes you a moment to understand that you have been refused.
  $ _asked += 1
  -> :room_ask
* Move the screen from the air shaft, and let the afternoon in [once] ?if not b1.general_knows
  There is a shaft in the corner of the ceiling, narrow as a sword, that goes up through the house to a grating in the garden. Hanno has screened it with a sheet of felt. You take the felt down.
  A single bar of light falls through the dust, white and cruel, across the rug.
  The General draws his feet up on the couch with the quick shy movement of a man who has been burned. Meritamun has gone very still. And you walk forward and put your face into it, and your hands, and stand there in the afternoon with the whole of the old fever creeping over your skin, as plain as a man standing in the rain.
  It is a long time. It is perhaps the length of three breaths. The General does not say anything. He looks at you, and you can see him thinking, with enormous speed and enormous care, like a man laying out a game of draughts, and when he has finished he lowers his eyes to his cup.
  "Put the felt back," he says gently. "It is a kindness to an old man." And then, as you do it: "I shall not ask. There are questions of which one should not like to know the answer, at my age and in my condition."
  $ b1.general_knows = true
  $ hunger += 4
  $ secrecy -= 2
  $ bond.general += 2
  Meritamun writes nothing for some little time. When the reed begins to move again, she is writing very small.
  -> :room_ask
* That is enough. Take your leave
  "Come again," says the General, with perfect courtesy. "I am not, at present, going anywhere."
  ~> room_out
