# room_out
You go up the stair, and the oak door shuts behind you, and the bolt goes home with a sound that it has not been made to make in a very long time. Hanno is waiting at the top with the tablet. He does not say a word. He puts a small dry biscuit into your hand, and goes away.
<~

# study
## The Merchant lady
$ _study += 1
$ do meet("prisca")
Prisca has had her study stripped. There is a table, and a chair, and the abacus. The scrolls have gone, and the bronze statuettes, and the Greek lamps that she has been proud of for two hundred years, and she is sitting very upright with her hands on the beads like a pilot on a tiller.
She says, before you have sat down, "I will tell you what I have done, so that you will not have to ask. I have fed him nothing. I have given him a roof and a couch and a sheep, and I have said to every vampire in this city who has put a question to me that I did not, in any sense, give him *drink*." She looks at you steadily. "Do you know what the custom is?"
You say that you do not.
"It is old," says Prisca. "It is much older than the Senate, and it is the only part of the law that the Senate did not invent. When one of us comes out of the dark, by whatever door, there is a moment when he is neither the Senate's subject nor any line's, and he is nobody's. The first person to give him drink, of their own blood or their own hand, is his *surety*. The Senate asks him no questions. It asks them of his surety. If he does a thing, it is the surety who is brought to the quarry and made to answer." She does not smile. "I am a banker. I do not hold what I cannot lend. I have been very careful."
:study_ask
* Ask her what the Senate will do [once]
  "It has called the lines to the old quarry at the second watch tomorrow," says Prisca. "It will hear what the lines wish. It will then do what Tarrax tells it. I am not sure that the Senate has ever done anything else." She turns a bead. "It would like him sealed. I think that is a thing that you have known since the quarry. I think that it is also a thing that the Senate cannot say aloud, because it was a decision that the Senate took two thousand years ago, and a great many people have a stake in its having been a good one."
  $ _seen += 1
  -> :study_ask
* Ask her what the Merchant house will pay to be left out [once]
  She looks at you for a long moment with an expression that you would call, in anyone else, admiration. "Everything that I have," she says. "Within reason. I will give a great deal to be a house that was not at the door." She names the sum. It would be a great sum to a king. "I would also like it to be understood," she says, "that I will not buy anyone's safety with yours."
  $ _seen += 1
  $ bond.prisca += 1
  -> :study_ask
* Ask her about Tarrax [once]
  "He is the oldest thing in the Senate's pocket," says Prisca, "and the only one that the Senate is afraid of. It does not know it. He has never made a mistake in my hearing, and I have heard him a great many times. I would say that it is a considerable strain on the man." She hesitates. It is a very small movement, a thing like the lifting of a bird's wing. "I have noticed that he has not slept in a house since the fire. He has been sitting in the Senate's rooms with the shutters closed. Galerius says that he is writing letters, and that he has burned all of them."
  $ _seen += 1
  -> :study_ask
* Ask her whether she will be at the quarry tomorrow [once]
  "Of course," says Prisca. "I am the only person in the city who can add." And then, more quietly, with the first real softening that you have seen in her face since the fire: "I did not expect him to be so civil. I had made up my mind to be very frightened. It is a thing that one prepares for and is then deprived of, and I find that I am not sure which I prefer."
  $ bond.prisca += 1
  -> :study_ask
* That is enough. Take your leave
  She nods, once, and goes back to her beads.
  ~> study_out
  <~
-> :study_ask

# study_out
Her door closes behind you, and you stand for a moment in the passage with the whole of the great, anxious, quiet house all round you, the ledgers and the strongboxes and the four hundred souls. It is a very fragile thing. It has been burned once. It has had, you think, a great deal of practice at being frightened and a great deal less at being brave, and it is, on the whole, holding up.
<~

# caelian
## The Senate's clerk
$ _seen += 1
$ do meet("galerius")
You go up the Caelian by the long way, through the new streets, and you are met at the gate of the barracks by a very small, very correct clerk with ink on his thumb, who is plainly not a vampire and plainly has not been told what to expect. He conducts you across the courtyard with his head down. Galerius is waiting in the shade at the far side, in his plain brown tunic, with his hands behind him.
?if b1.galerius == "hostile"
  He does not step forward. He says, in a flat voice, as though reading from a card, "The Senate of Blood will sit in the old quarry at the second watch tomorrow. The guest of the Merchant house will be produced. His surety will speak." He hands you the tablet. His hand is quite steady. He has not looked at your face.
?elif b1.galerius == "wary"
  He holds the tablet out to you a little stiffly. "The Senate of Blood sits in the old quarry at the second watch tomorrow," he says. "The guest will be produced, and his surety will speak. I was told to say it in those words. I would like it noted that I said them." He does not quite look at you.
?else
  He holds the tablet out to you with both hands, in the old manner, and there is something in his face that is very nearly an apology. "The Senate of Blood sits in the old quarry at the second watch tomorrow," he says. "The guest will be produced. His surety will speak. I was told to say it in those words, and I have."
?end
You take the tablet. It is cold. It has the Senate's seal on it, a small red wolf, and when you break it, it contains nothing that Galerius has not already said.
:caelian_ask
* Ask him what the Senate will do [once]
  He is silent for the space of a breath. Then he turns his head very slightly, as a man might to avoid being overheard, and he says, in a voice so low that the clerk cannot hear it, "There is a cell." He says it as a man gives the name of the dead. "It is under the Senate house. It has been there for a great while. It is sixty beams of cedar, bound with iron, and they have taken it out of the ground and they have had it oiled." He looks at the paving. "I have been shown it. I should like you to know that I did not enjoy being shown it."
  $ _seen += 1
  -> :caelian_ask
* Ask him about the third post at the narrow place [once] ?if b1.galerius_aid
  Something in his face lets go. It is a very small thing, and it is the most human that you have ever seen him. "Placidus," he says. "He has been moved to the kitchens, which is a punishment, and has been given a poultice, which is a kindness, and he is a good deal better. I have been told that he told his mother that a man in a grey cloak had asked his name, and that she is not sure whether to be frightened for him or proud." He clears his throat. "I should be obliged if nobody were to tell her."
  $ bond.galerius += 1
  $ _seen += 1
  -> :caelian_ask
* Ask whether he will be there tomorrow [once]
  "I am the Senate's officer," says Galerius. "It is a thing that I have been for a great many years, and I have made my peace with it." He considers. "I will tell you that I would rather be in Capua. It is not a thing that I would say to anyone else. I would be glad if you would not repeat it."
  $ _seen += 1
