# caelian_out
You go down the hill in the last of the long light. The new streets are very white and very empty, and the scaffolds stand against the sky like the ribs of some enormous beast. It is a city that is waiting. You can feel it in the cobbles. You find that you have your hand on the little wax tablet, in your sleeve, with the red wolf on it, and that you are holding it hard enough to leave a mark.
<~

# lane
## The General's people
$ _lane += 1
$ do meet("theron")
They have not gone. It is hot in the lane, in the thin stripe of shade by the wall, and they sit with their backs against the brick and their knees drawn up, in plain grey, without arms. Some of them have put their hoods over their faces against the glare. They have the wan and watchful look of men who have been asked to stay awake through a very long afternoon.
Theron is sitting at the end, on an upturned bucket, with a cloak round his shoulders and his ruined ear toward you. He is paler than you have ever seen him, and he is quite steady. He is, you think, a man who has done his weeping and is now at some pains to look as if he had not.
?if b1.door_side == "theron"
  "I have not forgotten," he says, "who stood with us." It is said gruffly, and it is said to the road.
?elif b1.door_side == "tarrax"
  "I have not forgotten," he says, "who stood with the other." It is said without heat. He has the air of a man who is making a note.
?else
  "I have not forgotten," he says, "where you stood." It is said carefully. He is a man who has not yet decided what it is that he has not forgotten.
?end
Behind him, a stocky woman with a shaved head and a scar through her lip has got to her feet, and is looking at you without any expression at all. It is the captain. She has not been given a name that you have heard. She does not seem to want one.
:lane_ask
* Ask Theron what the line wants [once]
  "Him," says Theron simply. "At home. In a house on the Aventine that we have kept clean for a hundred and ninety years, with a bed in it that no one has ever slept in." He looks at his hands. "I do not say that he will love us. I think that he will be disappointed. I think that he will be disappointed in a way that I can bear."
  $ _seen += 1
  -> :lane_ask
* Ask whether the line will fight the Lictors for him [once]
  "Yes," says Theron. He says it with no more heat than a man agreeing that it is Tuesday. "Not because we would win. Because if he is put back, I am an old man who has stood beside a door for as long as I have been anything, and has seen it open and shut again, and I would rather be dead." He turns his one whole ear to you. "I am telling you so that you will know what it is that you are asking of us, if you ask."
  $ _seen += 1
  $ bond.theron += 1
  -> :lane_ask
* Ask him to bring the General out alive [once]
  He stares at you. "That is what we are for," says Theron. He is a good deal affected, and it is a thing that he is not accustomed to being. The captain with the scar has moved, very slightly, to stand between you and the light.
  "It will be done," she says, in a voice like a file. "If it is the last thing that is done in that quarry."
  $ bond.theron += 1
  -> :lane_ask
* Ask the captain what she counted on the hill [once] ?if b1.company == "hunters"
  She looks at you. It is the first time that she has looked at you as if you were a person, and not a position. "Twenty two cloaks, and a boy with a jar who ought not to have been there, and one old man with a lamp," she says. "I told Theron what I counted. I did not tell him what I thought of it." She glances at him. "It is a thing that I have not said to him yet."
  She does not say it to you either. But she nods, once, very slightly, as to a person who has been given a reason to be left alone.
  $ bond.theron += 1
  -> :lane_ask
* That is enough. Take your leave
  Theron lifts a hand, and lets it fall. "It is a small thing to say," he says, "and it is all that I have. I am glad that it was you."
  ~> lane_out
  <~
-> :lane_ask

# lane_out
You go back down the lane between the two sides of the dark, and they watch you, the grey and the red, with their faces turned. Nobody speaks. The goat, which has been tied to a ring by the Merchant house door for most of the afternoon, regards you with deep and measured contempt.
<~

# dusk
## The first drink
The sun goes. It goes in the long, slow, gold fashion of Roman evenings, with a swallow in it, and Hanno comes to find you in the passage with the tablet held against his chest.
"He has asked for you," says Hanno. "He has asked for no one else."
The strongroom has changed. They have lit lamps, a great many of them, in niches and on the table, and the brazier gives out some heat at last, and the room has taken on a kind of crowded and shadowy comfort, like a tent in a camp. Prisca is there, on a stool by the door. She has her hands folded. Meritamun is at the table. The scarred captain has been let in, and is standing against the wall, as straight as a spear, with her eyes on the floor, and Theron is beside her, on one knee. He has been told, evidently, to remain so.
The General has been waiting. He sits up very straight on the couch with the borrowed tunic pulled round him, and the lamps lie on the bones of his face. He looks like a very great king who has come to some small town to hear a petition and has been put up, for the night, in a cupboard.
"I have been a guest," he says, "for a day. It is a position of some honour. It is not an *indefinite* one." He folds his enormous hands. "I have not been given what I need. I have been given what was convenient. I do not complain. I am saying only that there is a kind of manners in these things, and that I should prefer, tonight, to be asked."
There is a silence. Prisca does not stir. In the corner of your eye you see her go perfectly white.
"I will not drink in this house from anyone in it," says the General, "who is not in the position to be asked. It is a thing that I would not do to a hostess. It would make her a surety, and she has been so careful." He smiles at her. It is a small and very kind smile, and it goes through Prisca like a blade. "I think, therefore, that I am going to need a friend."
:meal
* Offer him your wrist
  You roll back your sleeve. It is not a thing that you have thought about. It is a thing that you find that you are doing, with a hand that is a good deal steadier than you would have supposed.
  The General looks at your arm. It is a long look, and entirely without greed, and there is in it something that resembles reverence.
  "It is a great thing to be offered," he says. "I will try to be worth it."
  He is very gentle. It is the gentleness of the very strong, who have learned from the very long habit of their strength what a thing it is to be careless with it, and the draw is slow and cool and enormous, like a river taken in at a mouthful. You feel it in your knees. You feel it in the pit of your stomach, where the hunger sits. When he lifts his head, his lips are dark and his eyes are shut.
  He holds it in his mouth. He holds it there, as a man holds a sip of wine, for a long time.
  "Ah," says the General, very quietly. And then, after another long silence, in a voice that is hardly his at all: "Thank you."
  He does not say what he tasted. He does not open his eyes until Hanno has put a cloth in your hand.
  $ b1.general_meal = "wrist"
  $ b1.general_knows = true
  $ hunger += 12
  $ bond.general += 3
* Bring him a willing donor from the Merchant house
  There is a man in the passage, a clerk with a plain tunic and a bandaged wrist and a look of some embarrassment, who has been asked, by Prisca's steward, and who has been paid. You have him brought in. The General looks at him gravely. He says, "I should like to know your name." The clerk tells him. The General says it back to him, twice, so that he will know it was heard.
  It is very quiet. The clerk says afterwards, in the passage, to Hanno, that it was not at all what he had expected. He says that it was as if somebody had sung to him. He does not say what song.
