-> dusk_after

# dusk_after
## What he said
The General sits back. He has a little colour. It has come up under the parchment like the first light on a wall, and he looks, for the first time since the quarry, like a man who may be about to get to his feet.
"Now," he says, "I am going to tell you all what I am going to do, so that nobody may be surprised." He looks round the room, slowly, lamp by lamp. "I have been asked what I want by a great many people. I find that I do not know. It is a bad time to be asked. It is the worst possible hour. I am going to be asked it again, tomorrow, in front of the people who put me under the hill, or the people who have taken it upon themselves to be sorry about it, and I am going to answer it *once*."
He turns, with great slowness, and looks at you.
"I will answer to one person. In front of the Senate, and in the hearing of the lines, and I will abide by what that person says, for one night." It is said without any irony. "It will not be Prisca. She would be so fair that she would sell me. It will not be the Commander. He would put me back, and be very sorry. It will not be Theron, who would weep on it." There is a small, strangled sound from the floor. "It is to be you."
?if original("general", "by") == "hero"
  "You opened it," says the General. "It is an old custom that the one who opens the door is answerable for what comes through. I did not invent the custom. I was in a position to observe it being invented."
?elif original("general", "by") == "faction"
  "The Commander lifted the bar," says the General, "and the Commander would see me back in the ground by sunrise, which is a thing that I should find hard to bear in a friend."
?else
  "Theron's people took off the bar," says the General, "and Theron would let me do anything, which is exactly why I will not ask him."
?end
He studies you. The eyes have lost something of their terrible brightness, and have gained something that is not at all easy to look at, a warmth, as of a fire laid in a very large grate.
"You stood there," he says, "and you looked at me as if I were a *problem*. Not a god. Not a beast. I have been looked at in a great many ways. It was restful."
Nobody says anything. Prisca has not moved. Meritamun has her stylus across her knee and her eyes on the General's face, like a woman watching a bridge about to be built.
?if b1.general_meal == "wrist"
  And somewhere under all of it, in the pit of your stomach, there is a thing that you cannot name. It is not hunger. It is the sense of having been seen, in the dark, by something that does not need a light.
?end
"Go and rest, little one," says the General, as kindly as a grandfather. "You will need to be clever tomorrow. I shall try not to make it harder."
You go out into the passage. It is full of lamplight and of people who stand against the wall as you go by. At the end of it, Hanno has laid a hand against the doorframe, and he says, in a very low voice and a great hurry, as though it had cost him a year of silence: "The lady says that you may have anything in the house."
It is a small, dry, terrible thing to be offered. It is the most that she has.
